%[text] # OPT 212: Complex Functions in wave optics
%[text] Category summary: Complex numbers, amplitude-phase form, phase visualization, branch cuts, complex optical fields, plane waves, and superposition.
%[text] 
clear
close all
clc
format short
set(groot,'DefaultAxesFontSize',14,'DefaultTextFontSize',14, ...
    'DefaultLegendFontSize',14,'DefaultColorbarFontSize',14);
%%
%[text] ## Big picture of Fourier Optics
%[text] ![](text:image:8fb2)
%[text] A fourier transform connects the pupil fields to the fields in the focal plane.  Numerically, this becomes a fast Fourier transform (FFT).   
%[text] ## Fourier Decomposition and Numerical Fourier Transforms
%[text] Functions of finite value can be represented, over a chosen interval, as superpositions of plane-wave-like functions. A periodic function may be represented by a discrete plane-wave expansion:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(\\mathbf{r})=\\sum\_{m=1}^{N}a\_m\n \\exp(i\\mathbf{k}\_m\\cdot\\mathbf{r}).$
%[text] For a one-dimensional periodic function of period $\\Lambda$, the fundamental spatial frequency is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f\_0=\\frac{1}{\\Lambda}.$
%[text] The allowed wave vectors satisfy
%[text]{"align":"center"} $k\_m\\Lambda=2m\\pi,\\qquad k\_m=2\\pi m f\_0.$
%[text] The Fourier series is therefore
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(x)=\\sum\_{m=-N}^{N}a\_m\\exp\[i2\\pi(mf\_0)x\].$
%[text] Orthogonality over one period gives
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{-\\Lambda/2}^{\\Lambda/2}\n e^{i2\\pi(mf\_0)x}e^{-i2\\pi(nf\_0)x}\\,dx\n =\\Lambda\\delta\_{mn}.$
%[text] The coefficients are
%[text]{"align":"center"} $a\_n=\\frac{1}{\\Lambda}\\int\_{-\\Lambda/2}^{\\Lambda/2}\n g(x)e^{-i2\\pi(nf\_0)x}\\,dx.$
%[text] As $\\Lambda\\to\\infty$, the spacing between allowed frequencies approaches zero and the Fourier series becomes the continuous transform pair
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(x)=\\int\_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}G(f\_x)e^{i2\\pi f\_xx}\\,df\_x,$
%[text]{"align":"center"} $G(f\_x)=\\int\_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}g(x)e^{-i2\\pi f\_xx}\\,dx.$
%[text] The rectangle function is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\it{rect}(x)=\n \\begin{cases}\n 1, & |x|\\leq 1/2,\\\\\n 0, & |x|\>1/2.\n \\end{cases}$
%[text] Its transform is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathcal{F}\\{\\it{rect}(x)\\}\n =\\frac{\\sin(\\pi f\_x)}{\\pi f\_x}\n =\\it{sinc}(f\_x).$
%[text] For sampled data, a direct numerical Fourier transform has the form
%[text]{"align":"center"} $G(f\_j)\\approx\n \\Delta x\\sum\_k g(x\_k)e^{-i2\\pi f\_jx\_k}.$
%[text] The FFT evaluates the same kind of discrete transform much more efficiently at a regular grid of frequencies.
%[text] ## Fourier Decomposition
%[text] A periodic function can be represented as
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(x)=\\sum\_{m=-N}^{N}a\_m\\exp\[i2\\pi(mf\_0)x\],$
%[text] where $f\_0=1/\\Lambda$ is the fundamental spatial frequency. The orthogonality relationship is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{-\\Lambda/2}^{\\Lambda/2}\n e^{i2\\pi(mf\_0)x}e^{-i2\\pi(nf\_0)x}\\,dx\n =\\Lambda\\delta\_{mn}.$
%[text] Therefore,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $a\_n=\\frac{1}{\\Lambda}\\int\_{-\\Lambda/2}^{\\Lambda/2}\n g(x)e^{-i2\\pi(nf\_0)x}\\,dx.$
%%
%[text] ## The Continuous Fourier Transform
%[text] In the limit $\\Lambda\\to\\infty$, the frequency spacing approaches zero and the Fourier series becomes the transform pair
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(x)=\\int\_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\n G(f\_x)e^{i2\\pi f\_xx}\\,df\_x,$
%[text]{"align":"center"} $G(f\_x)=\\int\_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\n g(x)e^{-i2\\pi f\_xx}\\,dx.$
%[text] ## **The Continuous Fourier Transform and Fourier Optics**
%[text] The same Fourier formalism extends to two dimensions by allowing both the position and frequency variables to be vectors. The transform pair is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(\\mathbf{r})=\n \\int\\!\\!\\int G(\\mathbf{f})\n e^{i2\\pi\\mathbf{f}\\cdot\\mathbf{r}}\\,d^2f,$
%[text]{"align":"center"} $G(\\mathbf{f})=\n \\int\\!\\!\\int g(\\mathbf{r})\n e^{-i2\\pi\\mathbf{f}\\cdot\\mathbf{r}}\\,d^2r.$
%[text] In coordinates, $\\mathbf{r}=x\\hat{x}+y\\hat{y}$ and $\\mathbf{f}=f\_x\\hat{x}+f\_y\\hat{y}$, so
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{f}\\cdot\\mathbf{r}=f\_xx+f\_yy.$
%[text] For free-space propagation to the far field, the field is proportional to the Fourier transform of the aperture field:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $E(x,y,z)=\n \\int\\!\\!\\int E\_\\mathrm{aperture}(u,v)\n \\exp\\left\[-i2\\pi\\left(xu\n +yv\\right)\\right\]\\,du\\,dv.$
%[text] where $x$ and  $y$ are in units of $\\frac{\\lambda}{NA}$, $NA$ is the numerical aperture of the system, and $u$ & $v$ are in pupil coordinates.  
%[text] This is what we will simulate.  The last bit of information is how the discrete FFT relates to the continuous Fourier transform in units.  The basic relation is $\\Delta x \\Delta u = \\frac{1}{N}$ ,  where $N$ is the number of points in the window.   Alternately,  $\\Delta x  = \\frac{1}{N\\Delta u} = \\frac{1}{W}$ , where $W$ is the window size.   
%[text] ## Step one: Simulating the point spread function of a perfect optical system
%[text] 1. Define the number of points.
%[text] 2. Establish a range at least 10 times the diameter of the pupil (the pupil is a unit circle).
%[text] 3. Create a 2d meshgrid over that range.
%[text] 4. Use a logical variable to identify points inside the pupil (with radius \< 1).
%[text] 5. Set points outside the pupil to 0, inside to 1.
%[text] 6. Calculate the Fourier transform of the pupil.  (Amplitude squared = Irradiance)
%[text] 7. Define the units for the axes.
%[text] 8. Create an image to visualize the focus spot of the perfect system.  \\ \


Npts = 1024;  % points in the sampling of the pupil
W =   20;  % window size
range =  linspace(-W/2, W/2, Npts); % The pupil diameter is 1
% Create a meshgrid
[u,v] = meshgrid(range,range    );
%% Set the pupil field Puv to zero
Puv =  zeros(Npts, Npts) ; 
%% Define a radius and set points inside the pupil  1 
r2 =   u.^2+v.^2 ;  % square of the radius
lvp =   r2<1 ;  % points inside the pupil 
% 
Puv(lvp) = 1; 

figure %[output:5c20dc1b]
imagesc(range,range,Puv); %[output:5c20dc1b]
axis image %[output:5c20dc1b]
colormap(gray) %[output:5c20dc1b]
xlim([-W/5 W/5]) %[output:5c20dc1b]
ylim([-W/5 W/5]) %[output:5c20dc1b]
title('Pupil Image') %[output:5c20dc1b]
%[text] Your pupil image should look like this:  
%[text] ![](text:image:2056)
% Compute the Fourier transform of the pupil  
Eimage =   fftshift(fft2(fftshift(Puv)))   ;
figure %[output:0169afd0]
% Compute the irradiance
II =   abs(Eimage).^2       ;
% 
% % Calculate the coordinates for the focal spot
dx = 1/W;
xrange = dx.*(-(Npts-1)/2:Npts/2);
% 
% % display an image of the focal spot (point spread function)
imagesc(xrange,xrange,sqrt(II)); axis image %[output:0169afd0]
colormap(gray) %[output:0169afd0]
% % zoom in to see the point spread function
xlim([-3 3]) %[output:0169afd0]
ylim([-3 3]) %[output:0169afd0]
title('Point Spread Function') %[output:0169afd0]
%[text] Your PSF should look like this: 
%[text] ![](text:image:5cc3)
%%\
% Display two interfering beams
figure %[output:8141f0a4]
Puv(lvp)=0;
u1 = 20;
v1 = 20;
Puv(Npts/2 + v1,Npts/2 + u1)=1;
Puv(Npts/2 - v1,Npts/2 - u1)=1;
% 
% Now calculate the diffraction pattern (the Fourier Transform)
Eimage = fftshift(fft2(fftshift(Puv)));
II = abs(Eimage).^2;

imagesc(xrange,xrange,II); shading interp %[output:8141f0a4]
axis image %[output:8141f0a4]
colormap(gray) %[output:8141f0a4]
colorbar %[output:8141f0a4]
% % % zoom in to see the fringes
xlim([-5 5]) %[output:8141f0a4]
ylim([-5 5]) %[output:8141f0a4]
%[text] Your interference pattern should look something like this:
%[text] ![](text:image:7fb2)
% now create a random number of interfering beams

NN=100;

up = 2*rand([1 NN])-1;
vp = 2*rand([1 NN])-1;
ruv = sqrt(up.^2 + vp.^2);
lp = ruv<=1;
up = up(lp);
vp = vp(lp);
ra = 0.1;
for ij = 1:length(up)
    lap = sqrt((u-up(ij)).^2 + (v-vp(ij)).^2)<=ra;
    Puv(lap) = exp(1i.*2.*pi.*rand(1));
end

% Visualize the phase in the pupil 
figure %[output:5f9e9a07]
imagesc(range,range,angle(Puv)) %[output:5f9e9a07]
shading interp %[output:5f9e9a07]
axis image %[output:5f9e9a07]
colormap(jet) %[output:5f9e9a07]
colorbar %[output:5f9e9a07]
xlim([-W/5 W/5]) %[output:5f9e9a07]
ylim([-W/5 W/5]) %[output:5f9e9a07]


% Find the Fourier transform of the pupil and display the pattern 
Eimage = fftshift(fft2(fftshift(Puv)));
II = abs(Eimage).^2;

figure %[output:9407fb45]
imagesc(xrange,xrange,sqrt(II)) %[output:9407fb45]
axis image %[output:9407fb45]
colormap(gray) %[output:9407fb45]
colorbar %[output:9407fb45]
xlim([-10 10]) %[output:9407fb45]
ylim([-10 10]) %[output:9407fb45]
% Now display the phase


figure %[output:1ff0acc3]
imagesc(xrange,xrange,angle(Eimage)) %[output:1ff0acc3]
axis image %[output:1ff0acc3]
colormap(jet) %[output:1ff0acc3]
colorbar %[output:1ff0acc3]
caxis([-pi pi]) %[output:1ff0acc3]
xlim([-10 10]) %[output:1ff0acc3]
ylim([-10 10]) %[output:1ff0acc3]
